Subject: wasix: snapshot shared memory across native forks

A host freezes a guest by stopping every thread and forking natively,
and every clone must diverge from its template. Shared overlays break
that: a native fork preserves the parent's live mappings, so clones
would keep sharing the template's objects, and one another's writes.
Freezing now detaches live mappings into a SharedMemorySnapshot
holding a private copy of each shared object and its mappings; a
forked child calls reshare_after_fork to create clone-local backing
and replay the overlays before any guest thread resumes.

Each copy is sparse: it walks the source's extents with SEEK_DATA and
SEEK_HOLE and writes only the chunks that are not all zero, so the
snapshot and each clone's backing keep holes wherever the source held
zeros, whether never written or written as zeros. A guest has touched
little of a shared object it has just created, and every clone copies
it; a hole costs nothing until written. A filesystem that cannot
report holes gets a plain copy.

Each object-side registration records the host address its overlay
occupies, which freeze and replay remap with MAP_FIXED. The frozen
mapping only keeps the range mapped until a child replaces it, so it
is marked MADV_DONTFORK: a child inherits nothing of it. Recycling an
execution already clears its registrations, so a snapshot never
replays a stale mapping over an address a later execution reuses.

